Date: Sun, 26 Dec 93 13:01:06 -0800
From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: Speed of evolution/selection of traits in humans...

Carl Feynman:
 
> Theories of genetic differences in intelligence are taboo [...]
 
  An interesting book in this context is Stephen Jay Gould's _The_Mismeasure_
_of_Man_ (Norton, 1981).  Even if you aren't interested in the study of
human intelligence, this book is interesting for insightful illumination
of how science was done poorly -- and correctly -- in the investigation of
a difficult and subtle problem.
 
  In spring 1992 I took an upper-division anthropology course at San Jose
State University titled "Human Heredity and Evolution".  The instructor
spent part of one lecture discussing recent efforts to determine the
relative importance of heredity and environment in determining human
intelligence.  [LARGE caveat:  "Intelligence" probably is not a single
quantity; that is, it likely has to do with many different qualities
that might be influenced by heredity and environment in different degrees.
What most field workers have used as an instrument of measurement is the
Stanford-Binet "IQ" test, largely because there are lots of archived
test scores for it; however, essentially nobody thinks that this test
is a very "good" intelligence test.]
 
  This work tends to involve studying identical twins who have been
raised in different families, e.g., perhaps because they were put up for
adoption.  That way you get to look at genetically identical individuals
who were not subject to the same environment.  Notwithstanding -- said
our instructor -- there are lots of subtleties and biases to deal with;
the measurements would be subject to much scientific debate even if they
were not politically controversial.
 
  A lot of the published results have to do with variances in "IQ".  The
thinking is like this:  You measure the IQs of individuals drawn from
a pool in which quantity X varied widely, and then again from another
pool in which quantity X varied but little.  If the variance in measured
IQ is the same in both cases, then you conclude that quantity X does not
affect intelligence.  If the variance is wider in the first case than in
the second, you conclude that X does affect intelligence, and that the
wider variance is presumably because some individuals lucked out on the
kind of X they got, and ended up smart, whereas others got bad kinds of
X and ended up dumb.  This kind of study is useful when there is no
easy way to quantify "X" the way you would want to do if you were looking
for a numerical correlation between X and IQ.
 
  (Note that "variance" is a technical term in statistics; it is one of
several measures of the width of a bell-shaped curve, or other distribution.
It in fact is a measure of the square of the width of such a curve.)
 
  The way this kind of thinking was applied to the question of intelligence,
heredity and environment was to take X to be heredity and to use identical
twins separated at birth as pairs of individuals whose heredity was
identical.  What you do is tabulate the difference in IQ scores between
such separated identical twins, after they have grown up.  Then you go
back to the records of the same adoption agency, same time, same et cetera,
and tabulate the differences in IQ scores between random, unrelated single
individuals who were placed through that agency.
 
  Now:  If IQ scores depended only on heredity, then identical twins
separated at birth should have ended up equally intelligent notwithstanding
being reared in different environments, so their IQ scores should have been
identical for each pair of twins, within error of repeatability of the IQ
test itself.  Whereas, if IQ scores depended only on environment, then
the fact that some pairs of tested individuals were twins should not have
showed up in the distributions -- they should have looked the same as for
the non-twins.
 
  When all was said and done, the result that seemed to shake out was that
about 60 percent of the variance in IQ score differences was attributable
to heredity.  (The actual statistic used was
 
      (Variance for non-(identical-twins)) - (Variance for identical twins)
      ---------------------------------------------------------------------
                        (Variance for non-(identical-twins))
 
If the variance for identical twins is zero, then this number is 1.00,
whereas if that variance is the same as for non-twins, then the number
is zero.)
 
  NOTE that that is *not* the same as saying that 60 percent of your IQ
points come from heredity and the rest from environment -- a common
misinterpretation by the innumerate.

  Our instructor left the clear impression that even if there were no
political controversy about these results, most of the scientific community
would regard them as tentative, because of the subtleties of the 
measurements.

  A little more history:  The first modern measurements of this kind were
reported by Jensen in 1971 who gave 80 percent.  His results have not been
repeated; however, the 60 percent number seems more robust.

  I hasten to add that I do not know enough about this matter to take a
position on it, or to elaborate upon the work reported.  I am only
repeating what was said in class, because I think Extropians may be
interested.
