Subject: Social Evolution

*
"Social evolution" is an ongoing process, but society seems to have the
tropic responses of an amoeba (ok, perhaps paramecium). It seeks food and
reacts when poked (i.e., money and dissent). Instead of starving people on
welfare I'd prefer to starve the system that supports them. 

We can all come up with wish lists of what would make for a better society:
decriminalize "victimless crime," stop victim disarmament, support
state-free schooling, end discrimination against employers, mandatory
prison for all police convicted of any crime, .... These are appealing
ideas, ones I support, but they are not really actionable. None of these
carries incentives to influence people currently with legislative power.

So, I prefer mechanisms that reduce the amount of money the government has
available. Of course, private barter, cash transactions, or even anonymous
transactions go in this direction. Tax revolt has some appeal. Voting
against incumbents, thereby attempting to create constant churn in the
system's participants also appeals to me. 

Santa Clara County, State of California and Uncle Sugar are among the
sticky fingers poking in my wallet.  While I'm not a fan of state power
(e.g., CA, NY), I prefer it to Fed power, but would prefer as many
decisions by others that affect me to be made as close to home as possible.


Yes, governmental apologists quickly point to the wonderful things
governments do (rail systems, highways, street lights, sewers, ...). Nice
things; use 'em myself. But these things come at a striking costs due to
"entitlements" or "surcharges." I'd like a balance where no government
payments are made to individuals, corporations or other governmental
agencies/jurisdictions unless it is payment for an explicit work contract,
all details and results of which are open to public inspection. 

And every time an elected official votes for any form of entitlement, a
thousand consitutents take a pledge to vote the bum out, irrespective of
other issues. (Admitedly, I'm a dreamer too).



